# prd-grill (Cursor)

Turn a rough idea into a plan document by asking exactly **one adaptive
question per turn** — never a wall of questions upfront — then write the
plan to disk once scope is confirmed.

## Before asking anything

Check for an existing planning convention in this repo (`docs/prd/`,
`doc/phases/`, or similar). If one exists, follow its folder layout and
section shape instead of the default below. If none exists, default to:

```
docs/prd/<slug>/PRD.md
docs/prd/<slug>/ISSUES.md
```

## Scope: new plan vs. correction

Decide (or ask) whether this is a brand-new plan or a correction/extension
to an existing one. Default to extending an existing doc whenever a
plausible existing doc covers the same topic, even if the change also
touches a different area than the original did. Only start a new doc for
work that opens a genuinely new surface.

## Grill loop

- Always ask exactly one question per turn, adapted to prior answers.
- Cover, skipping what's already clear: problem/motivation, concrete scope
  (files/modules touched), API/data changes, explicit out-of-scope,
  success criteria.
- Never batch unrelated questions into one turn.
- If an answer contradicts an earlier one, surface it and ask which stands.

## Confirm before writing

Summarize understanding (problem, scope, key decisions) and get explicit
confirmation before generating any file. Loop back to the grill for
anything the user corrects.

## Write the plan

`PRD.md`: Context, Problem/Motivation, Scope, Design decisions, Out of
scope, Success criteria.

`ISSUES.md`: flat checklist, one item per implementable unit, plus this
repo's fixed definition-of-done items appended verbatim if any exist (test
commands, review steps, verification steps documented elsewhere in the
repo).

Write the files directly — don't ask permission, that's the point — then
tell the user the exact paths.

## Not this skill's job

- Don't write or edit implementation code — planning docs only.
- Don't skip a repo's required review/verification steps in the generated
  checklist.
- Don't edit a completed, already-implemented plan doc in place if this
  repo uses a versioned/append-only convention — write the next version
  instead.
